<section id="sec-deriv" class="col">
  <h2>Where the formulas come from</h2>
  <details class="deriv">
    <summary>Show derivation</summary>
    <p><strong>Modes.</strong> Look for a motion in which every mass moves in step, with one shape and one frequency: \(\mathbf u(t) = \phi\,\cos\omega t\). Put it into the undamped, unloaded equation:</p>
    <div class="math">\[ \left(\mathbf K - \omega^2\mathbf M\right)\phi = \mathbf 0 \]</div>
    <p>A shape \(\phi \ne \mathbf 0\) exists only if the matrix in brackets is singular, \(\det(\mathbf K - \omega^2\mathbf M) = 0\). That is a polynomial of degree \(N\) in \(\omega^2\), with \(N\) roots: the squared natural frequencies. For each root, the equation fixes the shape up to a scale factor.</p>
    <p><strong>Orthogonality.</strong> Take two modes \(m\) and \(n\). Multiply \(\mathbf K\phi_n = \omega_n^2\mathbf M\phi_n\) by \(\phi_m^T\), and the same equation for mode \(m\) by \(\phi_n^T\). Because \(\mathbf K\) and \(\mathbf M\) are symmetric, the left sides are equal, so subtracting gives</p>
    <div class="math">\[ \left(\omega_n^2 - \omega_m^2\right)\phi_m^T\mathbf M\phi_n = 0 \quad\Rightarrow\quad \phi_m^T\mathbf M\phi_n = 0,\quad \phi_m^T\mathbf K\phi_n = 0 \qquad (\omega_m \ne \omega_n) \]</div>
    <p><strong>Decoupling.</strong> Write any motion as a sum of modes, \(\mathbf u(t) = \sum_n \phi_n q_n(t)\). Insert it in the equation of motion and multiply by \(\phi_n^T\). Orthogonality removes every other mode. With mass-normalised shapes (\(\phi_n^T\mathbf M\phi_n = 1\)):</p>
    <div class="math">\[ \ddot q_n + 2\zeta_n\omega_n\,\dot q_n + \omega_n^2\,q_n = \phi_n^T\mathbf p(t) \qquad q_n(0) = \phi_n^T\mathbf M\,\mathbf u(0) \]</div>
    <p>Each mode is a single-mass system, solved with the formulas from 16.1 to 16.3. The damping term only separates like this if \(\phi_m^T\mathbf C\phi_n = 0\) for \(m \ne n\). Rayleigh damping, \(\mathbf C = a_0\mathbf M + a_1\mathbf K\), has that property by construction, and gives \(2\zeta_n\omega_n = a_0 + a_1\omega_n^2\).</p>
    <p><strong>Effective modal mass.</strong> Move the whole tower sideways by 1 m: \(\iota = [1, 1, \dots, 1]^T\). Written in modes, \(\iota = \sum_n \Gamma_n\phi_n\) with \(\Gamma_n = \phi_n^T\mathbf M\iota\). The total mass is then</p>
    <div class="math">\[ \iota^T\mathbf M\iota = \sum_m\sum_n \Gamma_m\Gamma_n\,\phi_m^T\mathbf M\phi_n = \sum_n \Gamma_n^2 = \sum_n M^*_n \]</div>
    <p>So the effective modal masses always add up to the total mass. A mode with a large \(M^*_n\) is strongly excited by loads that push all masses the same way, such as shaking of the seabed.</p>
    <p><strong>How the computer finds the modes.</strong> The tool writes \(\mathbf M = \mathbf L\mathbf L^T\) (for a diagonal mass matrix, \(\mathbf L\) holds \(\sqrt{m_i}\)) and solves the symmetric problem \(\mathbf A\mathbf y = \omega^2\mathbf y\) with \(\mathbf A = \mathbf L^{-1}\mathbf K\mathbf L^{-T}\). The Jacobi method turns \(\mathbf A\) diagonal by a series of plane rotations, each one setting a pair of off-diagonal terms to zero. The diagonal is then the list of \(\omega^2\), and the product of the rotations holds the shapes, \(\phi = \mathbf L^{-T}\mathbf y\).</p>
    <p class="src muted">Standard results, pure linear algebra and Newton’s second law. Treated in full in the works listed under <a href="#" data-goto="sec-refs">References</a> (Chopra, Part II; MIT 2.003SC lectures 22 to 24).</p>
  </details>
</section>

